---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import { TOOLS, SITE, CATEGORY_ORDER, toolsByCategory } from '../data/tools';

const tool = TOOLS.find((t) => t.href === '/about')!;
const allTools = TOOLS.filter((t) => !t.content);
---

<Base title={tool.title} description={tool.meta} tool={tool} keywords={tool.keywords}>
  <!-- ============================ Hero ============================ -->
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="hero__text">
      <p class="label label--accent">Reference · About this site</p>
      <h1>About Sound Generator</h1>
      <p class="hero__lead">
        Sound Generator is a set of audio test tools that run entirely in your browser. Every tone, sweep and noise
        signal is synthesised on your device with the Web Audio API. There is no server, no account and no upload.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="hero__aside">
      <div class="notice notice--info">
        <span class="notice__icon" aria-hidden="true">i</span>
        <p class="notice__body">
          The site is free, has no ads and does not track you. If it is useful, the whole thing works offline once
          loaded.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== Long-form content ===================== -->
  <Panel eyebrow="Principles" title="What this site is">
    <div class="prose-tech">
      <h2>Design principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One page per job.</strong> Each tool does one thing and does it fully. The
          <a href="/">tone generator</a> makes a tone; the <a href="/sweep-generator">sweep generator</a> makes a
          sweep. Nothing is hidden behind a menu of modes.</li>
        <li><strong>Exact numbers.</strong> Every readout is the real figure: frequency in hertz, level in decibels,
          duration in milliseconds, wavelength in metres. No rounded marketing figures.</li>
        <li><strong>No accounts.</strong> Nothing to sign up for and nothing to log in to. Every setting lives in the
          URL, so a link is also a preset.</li>
        <li><strong>No ads.</strong> No advertising, no sponsorship and no affiliate links. There is nothing on the
          page competing with the tool.</li>
        <li><strong>Works offline.</strong> Audio is generated locally, so once a page has loaded it keeps working with
          no network connection.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        Every sound is built with the Web Audio API, the audio engine built into your browser. A short script creates
        an oscillator, a noise source or a sweep, routes it through a master gain and a safety limiter, and connects it
        to your output device. Nothing is fetched, streamed or rendered on a server.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site is a static set of pages. There is no application framework running in the browser, no server
        rendering and no database. Everything you see is generated at build time and shipped as plain HTML, CSS and a
        little JavaScript.
      </p>
      <h3>The offline WAV export</h3>
      <p>
        When you save a tone as a WAV file, the browser renders the signal offline with an
        <code>OfflineAudioContext</code>, the same maths as the live signal but faster than real time, then encodes the
        result as 16-bit PCM and hands it to your browser as a download. The file is built on your device. Nothing is
        uploaded to produce it.
      </p>
      <h3>The limiter and the master level</h3>
      <p>
        Every signal passes through one shared master gain and one shared limiter. The master gain is the slider in the
        header; it sets the output level for the whole site and is remembered between visits. The limiter is a safety
        net that catches signals that would otherwise clip in the digital domain. It is not a mixing tool, and it does
        not protect your ears: only a sensible level does that.
      </p>

      <h2>What the tools can and cannot tell you</h2>
      <p>
        These are signal generators and simple measurement aids, not calibrated instruments. Be clear about the limits.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No medical diagnosis.</strong> The <a href="/hearing-test">hearing test</a> steps through
          frequencies and logs what you report. It cannot measure hearing thresholds, cannot account for the level your
          device actually produces, and is not a substitute for an audiologist.</li>
        <li><strong>Your playback device is the real limit.</strong> Built-in laptop speakers cannot reproduce 40 Hz.
          Most headphones roll off above 16 kHz. If you cannot hear a tone, it may be the speaker, not your ear.</li>
        <li><strong>kHz limits come from the sample rate.</strong> The browser runs at its own sample rate, usually
          44.1 or 48 kHz. That sets a hard ceiling at the Nyquist frequency, half the sample rate, above which no
          correct signal can be produced.</li>
        <li><strong>Browser mixers can resample.</strong> Your operating system or a Bluetooth codec may resample or
          compress the audio before it reaches the speaker. Frequency accuracy is preserved, but absolute level and the
          very highest frequencies may not be.</li>
        <li><strong>Frequency is precise, level is not.</strong> The oscillator frequency is exact to the accuracy of
          your sound card clock, normally better than 0.01 %. The level you hear depends on the whole chain and cannot
          be read from this site.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who it is for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Musicians</strong> tuning instruments against a <a href="/instrument-tuning">reference pitch</a> or
          checking a note by ear.</li>
        <li><strong>Engineers and technicians</strong> testing speakers, drivers, crossovers and room response with
          <a href="/sweep-generator">sweeps</a> and <a href="/noise-generator">noise</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Teachers</strong> demonstrating interference, beats and harmonics, with the physics on the
          <a href="/theory">acoustic theory</a> page.</li>
        <li><strong>Installers</strong> checking telephone and DTMF signalling with the
          <a href="/dtmf">DTMF generator</a> and <a href="/pips">time pips</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Students</strong> learning what a frequency, a wavelength or a decibel actually sounds like.</li>
        <li><strong>Anyone</strong> who needs to test a speaker, a set of headphones or a hearing range.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Build note</h2>
      <p>
        The site is built with <strong>Astro</strong> and <strong>Tailwind CSS</strong> and deployed as static files.
        There are no accounts, no advertising, no cookie banner and no behavioural tracking. Every page is served from
        this domain; the one exception is Google Analytics, which measures page views in aggregate so that whoever pays
        for the hosting can see which tools are worth keeping. It is loaded in production builds only, it is skipped
        entirely if your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, and the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> describes exactly what it does and how to block it.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions, bug reports and corrections are welcome. Write to
        <a href="mailto:hello@tehnika.mk">hello@tehnika.mk</a>. The address is also listed in plain
        text as <code>hello@tehnika.mk</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        How your data is handled is set out in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <!-- ===================== Site index ===================== -->
  <Panel eyebrow="Index" title="Everything on the site">
    <div class="prose-tech">
      <p>
        Every tool on the site, grouped by category. This doubles as a full index of the pages that generate sound.
      </p>
    </div>
    {
      CATEGORY_ORDER.map((category) => {
        const items = toolsByCategory(category).filter((t) => !t.content);
        if (items.length === 0) return null;
        return (
          <div class="index-group">
            <p class="label index-group__title">{category}</p>
            <div class="card-grid">
              {items.map((item) => (
                <a class="card" href={item.href}>
                  <p class="card__t">{item.title}</p>
                  <p class="card__d">{item.tagline}</p>
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        );
      })
    }
    <p class="hint">
      {allTools.length} tools in total, plus the <a href="/theory">acoustic theory</a> reference and this page.
    </p>
  </Panel>

  <!-- ===================== Content pages ===================== -->
  <Panel eyebrow="More" title="Reference and legal pages">
    <div class="card-grid">
      <a class="card" href="/theory">
        <p class="card__t">Acoustic Theory</p>
        <p class="card__d">The maths behind the tones: sine waves, harmonics, decibels, wavelengths and sample rates.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/sitemap">
        <p class="card__t">Sitemap</p>
        <p class="card__d">Every tool and page on the site, grouped by what it does.</p>
      </a>
      <a class="card" href="/privacy">
        <p class="card__t">Privacy Policy</p>
        <p class="card__d">No accounts, no uploads and no audio leaves your device. It also covers the one thing that does leave: aggregate page-view statistics.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <p class="hint">Site email: <a href="mailto:hello@tehnika.mk">{SITE.email}</a></p>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.5fr) minmax(260px, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    align-items: start;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.2vw, 2.1rem);
    margin: 6px 0 8px;
  }
  .hero__lead {
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14.5px;
  }
  .notice__body { margin: 0; }

  .index-group { margin-top: 16px; }
  .index-group__title { margin-bottom: 8px; }

  .hint {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    font-size: 11.5px;
    color: var(--color-ink-4);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .hint a { color: var(--color-signal); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 2px; }
</style>
